### What is the default (weak) lifecycle for hidden classes?

Normal classes are reachable from their defining class loader, so they are only unloaded when the entire loader becomes unreachable. Hidden classes change this. By default (no `STRONG` option), a hidden class is only weakly held by the runtime, meaning that once there are no strong references to its `Class` object or its instances, it can be garbage collected and unloaded independently.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class WeakHiddenClassDemo {
    public void generatePerRequest() throws Exception {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5000; i++) {
            byte[] classBytes = generateValidatorBytes(i);

            // Default: no STRONG option = weak = independently unloadable
            Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                    .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true);

            Object validator = hidden.lookupClass()
                    .getConstructor().newInstance();
            // Use the validator for this request...
            // After this iteration, no strong ref remains
            // The hidden class is eligible for GC + unload
        }
        System.out.println("5000 validators created and eligible for GC");
    }

    private byte[] generateValidatorBytes(int id) {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder for ASM-generated bytecode
    }
}
```

This is the memory win: frameworks generating many short-lived classes do not accumulate them in metaspace.

### What does the STRONG option do?

The `STRONG` option changes the lifecycle. A strong hidden class is kept alive as long as its defining class loader is alive, exactly like a normal class. You choose `STRONG` when the hidden class is cached and reused many times, and regenerating it would waste CPU.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class StrongHiddenClassDemo {
    private Class<?> cachedCalculator;

    public void initCachedCalculator() throws Exception {
        byte[] classBytes = generateInterestCalcBytes();

        // STRONG: lives as long as the class loader
        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.STRONG);

        cachedCalculator = hidden.lookupClass();
        System.out.println("Cached calculator created: "
                + cachedCalculator.isHidden());
        // This class will NOT be unloaded until the loader is collected
    }

    private byte[] generateInterestCalcBytes() {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### When should you use weak vs STRONG?

The decision depends on the lifecycle of the generated class.

| Scenario | Option | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Per-request validator | Default (weak) | Discarded after each request, prevents metaspace leak |
| Per-trade rule class | Default (weak) | Thousands generated, each used once |
| Cached interest calculator | `STRONG` | Reused for entire service lifetime, regeneration is wasteful |
| Cached serialization proxy | `STRONG` | Shared across all requests, stable |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LifecycleDecision {

    // Per-request: use weak (default)
    public Object createTransferValidator(byte[] classBytes) throws Exception {
        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true);
        return hidden.lookupClass().getConstructor().newInstance();
    }

    // Cached singleton: use STRONG
    public Class<?> createCachedFeeEngine(byte[] classBytes) throws Exception {
        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.STRONG);
        return hidden.lookupClass();
    }
}
```

### What does the NESTMATE option enable?

The Java nest concept lets classes in the same nest access each other's private members directly. When you pass `NESTMATE`, the hidden class becomes a nestmate of the lookup class, so the hidden class can read and call the lookup class's private fields and methods, and vice versa.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NestmateAccessDemo {
    // Private field: only accessible to nestmates
    private BigDecimal feeRate = new BigDecimal("0.015");

    public void generateFeeCalculator() throws Exception {
        byte[] classBytes = generateCalcBytes();

        // NESTMATE grants private access to this host class
        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.NESTMATE);

        Class<?> calcClass = hidden.lookupClass();
        System.out.println("Nest host: " + calcClass.getNestHost().getSimpleName());
        System.out.println("Is nestmate of host: "
                + calcClass.getNestHost().equals(NestmateAccessDemo.class));

        // The generated class can now read 'feeRate' directly
    }

    private byte[] generateCalcBytes() {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        new NestmateAccessDemo().generateFeeCalculator();
    }
}
```

```text
Nest host: NestmateAccessDemo
Is nestmate of host: true
```

### Can you combine STRONG and NESTMATE?

Yes. The options compose. A cached proxy that must call private host methods uses both `STRONG` (long-lived) and `NESTMATE` (private access).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class ComposedOptionsDemo {
    private String encryptionKey = "NBI-SECRET-2024";

    public Class<?> createCachedProxy() throws Exception {
        byte[] classBytes = generateProxyBytes();

        // Both STRONG (long-lived) and NESTMATE (private access)
        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.STRONG,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.NESTMATE);

        return hidden.lookupClass();
        // This proxy lives with the loader and can read encryptionKey
    }

    private byte[] generateProxyBytes() {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### What is the metaspace impact in production?

In a banking microservice that generates rule classes per request, relying on the default weak behaviour avoids a slow metaspace leak that would eventually trigger `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. The `STRONG` option must be used carefully because strong hidden classes accumulate like normal classes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class MetaspaceDemo {
    public void showMetaspaceRisk() throws Exception {
        // DANGEROUS: STRONG with many short-lived classes
        for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
            byte[] classBytes = generateRuleBytes(i);
            MethodHandles.lookup().defineHiddenClass(
                    classBytes, true,
                    Lookup.ClassOption.STRONG // BAD: these never unload!
            );
        }
        // 100,000 classes stuck in metaspace until loader dies
        // Risk: OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace
    }

    public void showMetaspaceCorrect() throws Exception {
        // CORRECT: default weak for short-lived classes
        for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
            byte[] classBytes = generateRuleBytes(i);
            Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                    .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true); // default = weak
            Object rule = hidden.lookupClass().getConstructor().newInstance();
            // Use rule, then discard: GC can collect the class
        }
    }

    private byte[] generateRuleBytes(int id) {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### How does NESTMATE replace the old Unsafe host-class mechanism?

The old `Unsafe.defineAnonymousClass(hostClass, bytes, null)` had an implicit host-class parameter that gave the anonymous class access to the host's internals. `NESTMATE` formalises that same access through the specified nest-based rules, making it standard and auditable.

```java
// OLD (internal, removed):
// Class<?> old = Unsafe.getUnsafe()
//     .defineAnonymousClass(HostClass.class, bytes, null);
// old could access HostClass private members implicitly

// NEW (Java 15+, standard):
// Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
//     .defineHiddenClass(bytes, true, ClassOption.NESTMATE);
// hidden class can access host private members via nest rules
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In production, the choice between WEAK and STRONG determines whether your framework leaks metaspace. WEAK (the default) is correct for most use cases: generated proxies, lambda implementation classes, and script-compiled classes should be garbage-collected when no longer referenced. STRONG is reserved for classes that must outlive their defining class loader, such as JIT-compiled code or shared interop stubs. Monitor `jdk.ClassDefine` JFR events and metaspace usage to detect leaks.

```java
package com.bank.runtime;

public class HiddenClassProductionAdvice {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Production advice for hidden classes:");
        System.out.println("  Default (WEAK): class GC'd when unreferenced");
        System.out.println("    Use for: proxies, lambdas, script classes");
        System.out.println("    Monitor: metaspace should stay stable");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  STRONG: class lives as long as defining loader");
        System.out.println("    Use for: JIT code, shared interop stubs");
        System.out.println("    Risk: metaspace leak if loader is long-lived");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Monitoring:");
        System.out.println("    JFR event: jdk.ClassDefine (count hidden defines)");
        System.out.println("    JMX: java.lang:type=MemoryPool,name=Metaspace");
    }
}
```

